er view of the ledger-line heads (Bb4 or C5), would settle it."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5395,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5395},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4164e9-931f-4a24-85b5-7ae1d47d73af","uuid":"b36eb571-f7b3-446a-ae31-2979fdf8f0fb","timestamp":"2026-11-04T23:38:15.711Z","request_id":"req_011CgimUu7h8kUgGTuDffg6P","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Hf8Yqi5znwFAkgMppRqsPc":{"working":"The line opens with the C3 clef, three flats and an f. Bar 26: four beamed A4+F4 eighth chords, the first marked with a natural, which is the written-out tremolo half. Then an A4+F4 quarter and a quarter rest, and a barline. Only bar 26 has a quarter and a rest after the A/F chords, so the line starts there, not at bar 24. Bar 27: a whole-bar rest and a barline. Bar 28: two beamed groups of four eighths on and above the top of the staff, matching Bb4 Eb5 D5 C5 | Bb4 C5 Bb4 C5. Bar 29: a single quarter (Bb4), a quarter rest and a half rest. This is the distinctive bar that anchors the count. Bar 30: two more groups of four eighths, the same figure as bar 28. Then come two bars, each holding two open half notes on the first ledger line above the staff (Bb4), with a tremolo stroke through each stem, then the final barline. By position these are bars 31 and 32. But their notes don't match the edition's text. Bar 31 there is Bb4 then seven F4 eighths. Bar 32 is Eb4 and three Bb4 eighths, then a Bb4 tremolo half. The copy's tremolo Bb4 halves look more like bar 33. They don't match bars 33 and 34 either, because bar 34 is on C5 and all four of these heads sit on the same ledger line. So this copy probably has